\title{CISC vs RISC}

\p{Two design philosophies for an instruction set architecture.}

\transclude{RIS1}
\transclude{XJQO}

\problem{[CISC](RIS1) needs a fat [[87GH]] engine to translate its complex instructions into hardware-executable primitives. That's silicon area, power, and design complexity that doesn't directly add throughput.}

\solution{Once [RISC](XJQO) clearly won as a hardware model, x86 vendors adopted \em{instruction translation}: a [CISC](RIS1) front-end decodes x86 into RISC-like [[WEW2]] internally. The chip is [RISC](XJQO) on the inside, [CISC](RIS1) on the outside.}

\remark{The cost of legacy is real. The original Pentium spent roughly 30\% of its transistors on legacy support (mostly the microcode ROM). The Pentium Pro spent 40\% on backward compatibility. Modern x86 chips have absorbed this overhead because they can — transistor budgets grew faster than the legacy tax.}
